{
  "teamId": 144,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Atlanta Braves were a juggernaut from the jump, a team that seemed to manufacture runs in bunches and punish any opponent that dared to hang around. April set the table beautifully — Sean Murphy's two-run blast on April 10th announced the new catcher's arrival, and a pair of clutch singles by Travis d'Arnaud and Orlando Arcia on April 6th showed the kind of lineup depth that would define this squad all year. Even when A.J. Minter absorbed a brutal Yordan Alvarez homer and Jesse Chavez surrendered a costly two-run double late in the month, the Braves closed April at 17-9 and already looking like a division titan.\n\nMay wobbled — a 15-14 record masked the fact that a Raisel Iglesias blown save against Toronto in mid-May cost them dearly — but June became the month where Atlanta truly separated itself from the National League field. A scorching 21-4 run through June, anchored by Eddie Rosario's grand slam on June 4th, back-to-back home runs from Orlando Arcia and Ozzie Albies on June 8th, and a Matt Olson three-run bomb on June 25th, made the Braves look essentially unbeatable. This was the stretch that cemented their identity: a deep, powerful lineup that could hurt you from anywhere in the order.\n\nJuly and August maintained the relentless pace. Austin Riley's three-run walkoff blast in July, Ozzie Albies continuing his monster home run campaign, and Eddie Rosario providing thunderous contributions from the bench role kept the machine humming. August was their second-best month at 21-8. September brought some turbulence — Raisel Iglesias surrendered back-to-back home runs to the Phillies in consecutive days, and A.J. Minter was touched up again — but the Braves still managed 16 wins and arrived at October with 104 victories, one of the best records in baseball.\n\nThe postseason told a crueler story. The Philadelphia Phillies, battle-hardened and dangerous, matched Atlanta blow for blow in the NLDS. Austin Riley's two-run homer in Game 2 kept hope alive, and Travis d'Arnaud added a clutch shot of his own, but Bryce Harper proved an immovable force — homering multiple times across the series and haunting Atlanta's bullpen on multiple occasions. Spencer Strider and Max Fried both surrendered critical home runs, and the Braves' high-powered offense couldn't sustain the magic that had carried them through 162 games. A 104-win season ended not in triumph but in the quiet devastation of an early October exit.",
